## Plan

Eight stages. Each ends in something demonstrable. Ordering constraints that must
not be broken: **gates before writes**, **gates before AI**, **seam before
FreeCAD**.

### Stage A — Re-anchor and seam *(no new user-visible features)*

The enabling refactor. Same demo, architecture that can carry the rest.

1. **Resolve `satisfy` links.** In [extract.py](src/sysmlcad/extract.py) add
   `resolve_satisfies(elements) -> dict[requirement_def_id, part_usage]` walking
   the chain shown above. Note `_UPWARD_FIELDS` blocks `source` but not
   `memberElement`/`target`, so `reachable()` already permits it. Populate
   `Requirement.subject` with the real part name and add `subject_id`.
2. **Mapping schema v2** — `mapping/mapping.yaml`, two sections:
   - `parts:` — SysML `PartUsage` (qualified name + element id) ↔ CAD object ref
   - `attributes:` — SysML attribute ↔ CAD measurement or parameter, with
     `unit_sysml`, `unit_cad`, `direction` (`sysml_owns` | `cad_owns` | `both`),
     `tolerance`, `status` (`proposed`|`confirmed`|`disabled`), `author`,
     `confirmed_at`, `note`
   `direction` is the ownership rule the user asked for; nothing writes yet, but
   the field exists from day one so Stage G is a change of behaviour, not schema.
3. **`CadAdapter` protocol** — new `src/sysmlcad/cad/` package. Keep it minimal
   and honest: `state_id()`, `objects()`, `parameter(obj, name)`,
   `measure(obj, method, args)`, `material(obj)`. Move existing build123d code to
   `cad/build123d_adapter.py`; `material()` returns `None` there, and the caller
   reports the gap rather than substituting (FREQ-2's explicit requirement).
4. **Three verdicts + provenance.** In [model.py](src/sysmlcad/model.py): replace
   `Verdict.passed: bool` with `status: Literal["compliant","within_tolerance",
   "violated"]` plus `deviation`; add a `RunContext` (sysml project id, commit id,
   CAD `state_id`, mapping digest, timestamp, tool version). Update
   [tests/test_requirements.py](tests/test_requirements.py).
5. **Hygiene from G above**, plus: `pyproject.toml`, English throughout, CI on
   3.14 with `tests/**` in trigger paths, reconcile `models/motor_housing.sysml`
   with the notebook.
6. **Two cheap de-risking probes** (a day each, high information yield):
   - Extend the reference model to a **two-part assembly** (housing + cover) with
     an interface dimension and an assembly-level mass budget — the current
     single part cannot exercise part-level mapping at all.
   - Run `explore.py` over **`airbus/apollo-11-sysml-v2`** (ROADMAP §4.4). Expect
     "one comparison per requirement" to break first. Cheap evidence for which
     simplification to fix next.

### Stage B — Change-impact analysis *(FREQ-8, SCN-3)*

Pure read; the second pillar and the cheapest big win.
- Extend `SysMLClient` with `relationships(project, commit, element_id,
  direction)` and `changes(project, commit)`.
- `src/sysmlcad/impact.py`: given a changed element on either side, follow
  mapping entries plus SysML traceability links; return affected requirements and
  verification cases **each with its link path**. Mark results derived from an
  incomplete link structure as such, per FREQ-8.
- Demo: the SRS's own example — housing aluminium → plastic surfaces the mass
  budget and the material requirement.

### Stage C — Evidence log and reports *(SCN-4)*

- Append-only SQLite: `run` (RunContext) + `result` (one row per evaluated
  constraint). Nothing overwritten, nothing deleted.
- `report.py` → HTML (human) + JSON (machine), both stating which design state
  was checked against which requirement state. Keep the JUnit XML for CI.

### Stage D — MCP server *(demo milestone)*

Thin FastMCP wrapper over Stage A–C functions: `list_requirements`,
`verify`, `analyze_impact`, `generate_report`, `explain_mapping`. No logic of its
own. This is the slide-8/9 integration story, and the point at which an assistant
can be asked "what breaks if this housing becomes plastic?" and get a
deterministic answer.

### Stage E — Gates and human confirmation *(FREQ-5, ROADMAP §4.2)*

Dimensional compatibility (pint) · existence · uniqueness · plausibility
(order of magnitude) · human confirmation recorded with user and timestamp.
Result is *mapping valid/invalid*, reported separately from *pass/fail*. Required
before any write and before any AI proposal.

### Stage F — FreeCAD adapter *(FREQ-2 for real)*

Second `CadAdapter` implementation: document model, named parameters and
Spreadsheet, assembly tree, assigned materials. Unlocks three of the four
operational cases from the slides — budget tracking (mass summed over an
assembly), interface control (agreed dimension vs as-designed), substance
compliance (material + mass data). Keep `Build123dAdapter` as the offline CI
reference so the pipeline never needs FreeCAD.

### Stage G — Ownership-gated writes *(FREQ-3 `direction`, ROADMAP §5)*

- Writes are permitted only where the mapping entry's `direction` grants
  authority: SysML owns limits, CAD owns actual values.
- **Up:** actual values and verdicts back to SysML via
  `POST /projects/:id/commits`.
- **Down:** limits into CAD as parameters or annotations — **never automatic
  geometry changes** (ROADMAP §5.1).
- Every run has a dry-run preview showing the value changes it would make
  (SRS §4.1, Synchronization view), and every write lands in the evidence log.
- Defer **FREQ-6 structural instantiation** until this is stable; it is the most
  expensive item in the SRS and buys the least until assemblies work.

### Stage H — AI mapping proposals *(FREQ-4)*

`rapidfuzz` + embeddings over names and metadata, optionally an LLM with
structured JSON output. Candidates enter as `status: proposed` with confidence
and rationale; they are never applied. **Measure hit rate against a hand-mapped
gold set** — without that number there is no evidence the AI part saves time.

### Deferred, deliberately

SMT over the constraint network (ROADMAP §6 — the scoping there is correct);
web frontend, OAuth2, multi-project dashboard; CATIA/Creo adapters.

