Stage A is complete and verified. Here's where things stand.

Your questions
Are we on the right track? Yes. Three of the roadmap's own corrections to the source material are better than what the SRS and slides specify, and I'd defend all three: SMT belongs on the constraint network rather than B-Rep geometry; per-parameter ownership beats Operational Transformation; and mapping validity ≠ requirement satisfaction (absent from the SRS entirely, and the most valuable idea you had).

Is it good as a start? Yes — specifically because it's narrow. A working make verify → exit 1 → red pipeline is a real capability. The gaps were honest ones, documented in the code rather than hidden.

The one correction that was due now: the mapping sat at the wrong granularity (requirement → measurement), while both documents ask for part → CAD object, attribute → parameter. The link needed to fix it was already in your model and was being thrown away — satisfy maxLengthCheck by motorHousing serialises into a traversable chain that extract.py discarded in favour of the subject parameter (housing). Doing this after building a mapping store would have meant migrating the store.

What's now in place
extract.py:resolve_satisfies — requirements resolve to the real part (motorHousing) with its element id and a link path, which Stage B needs to cite.
adapters/ — CadAdapter seam; nothing above it imports a geometry kernel. Build123dAdapter stays as the offline CI backend; FreeCAD joins the registry without changes above.
Three-valued verdicts with deviation and tolerance; RunContext records the SysML commit, CAD state digest and mapping digest. The digests demonstrably change with the geometry (bdaee4 → dda854).
Gaps are not passes — an unmapped or unmeasurable requirement is carried and reported, never substituted (FREQ-2). A test asserts gaps == [].
Mapping schema v2 with a direction field; nothing writes yet, so Stage G is a behaviour change rather than a migration.
22 tests pass (was 6). All four acceptance criteria verified, plus the full engine driven over your real captured API payload.
One correction to what I told you earlier
docker/.env was never committed. I repeated a subagent's finding without checking it. The bare .env rule in .gitignore matches at any depth, so it was always covered. I kept the explicit docker/.env rule and added .env.example, but the secret rotation was unnecessary — harmless, the API picks up the new value at next start.

The missing docker/jupyter.Dockerfile was real: deleted in f3e8e18 as collateral alongside the API Dockerfile that the fork legitimately replaced. Restored from dde4b82, technically identical.

Blocked on infrastructure
Two Stage A items need make api on the host (the devcontainer has no Docker access, and sysml-api:9000 is unreachable): extending the reference model to a two-part assembly, and the Apollo-11 extractor load test. Both are recorded as open in ROADMAP.md.

One thing I left alone: out/elements.json and out/relevant.txt are tracked in git (~315 KB of debug dump). Useful as a captured reference payload — I used it for this whole analysis — so I didn't touch it, but it's your call.

Nothing is committed. If the gap-by-gap comparison against the SRS would be useful for your supervisors as a standalone document, I can publish it as a shareable page.

